    "# Quiz: Introduction to classification\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On Alps:** These are the questions of this lesson's pre-lecture and post-lecture quizzes, from the course's quiz app, with the answers the quiz app accepts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-1-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Classification is a form of supervised learning that has a lot in common with</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Time Series</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Classification is a form of supervised learning that has a lot in common with regression techniques.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Regression techniques</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Classification is supervised learning, and it has a lot in common with regression techniques.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>NLP</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Classification shares a lot with the regression techniques you used before, like linear and logistic regression.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-1-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>What question can classification help answer?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Is this email spam or not?</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Classification sorts data into classes. A basic example is deciding if an email is spam or not.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Can pigs fly?</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Classification answers questions by sorting data into classes, like sorting emails into spam or not spam.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>What is the meaning of life?</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Classification sorts data into classes, such as spam or not spam, and lets you ask questions of it.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-1-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>What is the first step to using Classification techniques?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>creating classes of a dataset</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Before you classify anything, the first task is to clean and balance your data.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>cleaning and balancing your data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. The first task is to clean and balance your data so you get better results.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>assigning a data point to a group or outcome</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Assigning data to a group or outcome is what the classifier does later. First you clean and balance the data.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-1-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>What is a multiclass question?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>the task of classifying data points into multiple classes</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. In a multiclass question, each data point fits one class, picked from several possible classes.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>the task of classifying data points into one of several classes</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A multiclass question picks one class out of several, like which of several cuisines a batch of ingredients fits.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>the task of cleaning data points in multiple ways</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Multiclass is about sorting data into one of several classes, not about cleaning data.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-1-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>It's important to clean out recurrent or unhelpful data to help your classifiers solve your problem.</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>true</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Recurrent data can cause confusion between classes, so you drop it, like the common rice, garlic and ginger.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>false</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. You should clean out recurrent data, like the most common ingredients, because it creates confusion between classes.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-4-1-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>What's the best reason to balance your data?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Imbalanced data looks bad in visualizations</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Balancing is about results, not looks. With uneven data, a model predicts the bigger class more often.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Balancing your data yields better results because an ML model won't skew towards one class</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. If most data is one class, the model predicts that class more often. Balancing removes this skew.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Balancing your data gives you more data points</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Balancing can create new samples, but the reason to do it is better results, with no skew toward one class.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
